  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "A temblor is an earthquake or a significant trembling of the ground. Derived from Spanish and widely used in the American West, it refers to the shaking caused by seismic activity along a fault or volcanic eruption. Common synonyms include tremor, earthquake, quake, and aftershock. Usage Examples: \"The magnitude 6.5 temblor caused minor damage in the city center.\" \"Aftershocks from the main temblor continued throughout the week.\" \"Residents were warned about potential temblors due to ongoing seismic activity.\" Key Details: Origin: The word comes from the Spanish verb temblar (to tremble) and gained traction in the US in the late 19th century. Pronunciation: Usually pronounced TEM-blor (tem-blər) or tem-BLOR. Common Error: It is often confused with \"tremblor,\" but temblor is the standard term, specifically used to avoid confusing it with \"trembler\".",
